  "answer_text": "The word **hosanna** has two primary definitions depending on its context: a literal, historical meaning and a modern, liturgical meaning. \n\n### 1. The Literal and Etymological Meaning\nHistorically, *hosanna* is a Hebrew phrase that translates literally to **\"save, please\"** or **\"save, we pray\"**. \n* It originates from the Hebrew words *yasha* (meaning \"to save\" or \"to deliver\") and *na* (a particle expressing entreaty or request, meaning \"please\" or \"now\"). \n* In the Hebrew Bible, it first appears in Psalm 118:25 as *h\u014d\u0161\u012b\u2018\u0101h-nn\u0101*\u2014a direct plea to God for salvation and help.\n\n### 2. The Liturgical and Modern Meaning\nIn modern English and Christian traditions, *hosanna* is used as a **cry of praise, adoration, or acclamation**. \n* Rather than a desperate plea for help, it is chanted or sung as a joyful declaration of trust and celebration. \n* In this context, it is similar to saying *\"Glory to God!\"* or *\"Hail to the Savior!\"*\n\n---\n\n### How the Meaning Evolved\nThe shift in the word's meaning from a \"plea for help\" to a \"shout of praise\" is tied to its famous biblical context in the New Testament:\n\n* **The Triumphal Entry (Palm Sunday):** When Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a donkey, crowds gathered, waving palm branches and shouting, *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!\"* (Matthew 21:9).\n* **The Transition:** By shouting \"Hosanna\" to Jesus, the crowd was both crying out for deliverance (\"Save us, Messiah!\") and simultaneously praising him as the long-awaited King who had arrived to bring that salvation. Over time, the praise aspect of the word became its primary association. \n\nToday, *hosanna* is frequently used in Christian hymns, Jewish prayers, and liturgical church services (such as during the Catholic Mass) as an expression of worship.",
